<file path="README.md">
# YatraSathi — JavaScript frontend + JavaScript backend

**Go further. Feel closer.** A hackathon travel companion for exploring India with place stories, practical planning, an interactive map, and honest emergency options.

## What this repository actually uses

This repository is **Next.js (App Router), React, JavaScript/JSX, PostgreSQL, and Drizzle ORM**. It is not the separate Vite folder described earlier. Every application source file is `.js` or `.jsx`; the backend is the JavaScript route handlers in `src/app/api/`. `next.config.mjs`, `drizzle.config.js`, `postcss.config.mjs`, and `eslint.config.mjs` are JavaScript configuration files. Next.js generates `next-env.d.ts` for its own tooling; it is not application code. `tsconfig.json` only lets the platform's validation command and `@/` aliases work with JavaScript (`allowJs: true`, `checkJs: false`).

**Why not Express yet?** A Next.js route handler already executes on the server in Node.js and can securely talk to PostgreSQL and an AI provider. Adding Express now would create a second server, deployment, and authentication boundary without improving this stage. If you later separate the services, the logic in `src/lib/` can move into an Express app without rewriting the UI.

```text
Browser (React .jsx)
   ├── /api/chat    → ai-provider.js → OpenAI (only if configured), otherwise labeled demo
   ├── /api/trips   → planner.js → Drizzle ORM → PostgreSQL
   ├── /api/nearby  → OpenStreetMap Overpass (may be unavailable)
   └── SOS          → device dialer/share/SMS (never claims automatic dispatch)
```

## Follow the code, one file at a time

| Purpose | Exact file path |
| --- | --- |
| Homepage route | `src/app/page.jsx` |
| Homepage interactions | `src/components/home-experience.jsx` |
| Shared navigation and actions | `src/components/site-shell.jsx` |
| Destination pages | `src/app/destinations/[slug]/page.jsx` and `src/components/destination-experience.jsx` |
| Editable trip planner | `src/app/plan/page.jsx` and `src/components/trip-planner.jsx` |
| Interactive map | `src/app/map/page.jsx` and `src/components/map-explorer.jsx` |
| Chat interface | `src/components/chat-drawer.jsx` |
| SOS interface | `src/components/sos-modal.jsx` |
| API: chat | `src/app/api/chat/route.js` |
| API: trips | `src/app/api/trips/route.js` and `src/app/api/trips/[id]/route.js` |
| API: map listings | `src/app/api/nearby/route.js` |
| API: health check | `src/app/api/health/route.js` |
| AI provider selection and demo | `src/lib/ai-provider.js` |
| Plan validation and suggestions | `src/lib/planner.js` |
| Database connection and table | `src/db/index.js` and `src/db/schema.js` |
| DB command configuration | `drizzle.config.js` |
| Interface translations | `src/lib/i18n.js` |
| Curated place data and sources | `src/lib/destinations.js` |

**Suggested reading order:** `src/app/page.jsx` → `src/components/home-experience.jsx` → `src/app/api/chat/route.js` → `src/lib/ai-provider.js` → `src/app/api/trips/route.js` → `src/db/schema.js`.

## Run in VS Code (Windows PowerShell)

Run each command in the **VS Code integrated PowerShell terminal**, from the repository root—the folder containing `package.json`. If you have copied this Next.js repository to `C:\Users\HP\Desktop\yatrasathihackathon`, first run:

```powershell
cd C:\Users\HP\Desktop\yatrasathihackathon
```

Check you are in the correct project:

```powershell
Get-Content .\package.json
```

The scripts should say `next dev` and `next build`, not `vite`. Use Node.js 20.9 or newer for Next.js 16. Check `node --version` and `npm --version` if startup fails.

1. Install the already-declared dependencies (not Nodemon):
   ```powershell
   npm install
   ```
2. Copy the environment template **only if you do not already have a `.env`**:
   ```powershell
   Copy-Item .env.example .env
   ```
3. Open `.env` locally and set `DATABASE_URL` to a **real PostgreSQL database you control**. The application reads this value on the server. Leave `OPENAI_API_KEY` empty for visibly labeled demo chat. If you have your own key, place it in `.env`, never in a `.jsx` file and never in a chat message. A local PostgreSQL server or a suitable hosted database must already exist; the app does not install PostgreSQL for you.
4. Apply the JavaScript Drizzle schema:
   ```powershell
   npx drizzle-kit push
   ```
5. Start both frontend **and** API routes together:
   ```powershell
   npm run dev
   ```
6. Open `http://localhost:3000`. You should see the destination homepage. `http://localhost:3000/api/health` should return `{ "ok": true }` if PostgreSQL is reachable.

To stop the dev server, press **Ctrl+C** in that terminal. To check a production build, run `npm run build`; do not confuse a successful Vite server at port 5173 with this Next.js app at port 3000.

## JavaScript backend API

All requests stay on your own origin. The browser uses `fetch('/api/...')`; route handlers access `process.env` on the server. Do **not** use a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable for a private AI key.

| Endpoint | Request | Real behavior |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GET /api/health` | none | Verifies a PostgreSQL query through Drizzle. |
| `GET /api/chat` | none | Returns `{ "mode": "demo" }` or `{ "mode": "live" }`. |
| `POST /api/chat` | JSON `messages`, optional `destination`, `language` | Validates and rate-limits input. Calls OpenAI Responses API from the server **only** if `OPENAI_API_KEY` exists; otherwise returns an explicitly labeled rule-based demo answer. It does not know live prices or schedules. |
| `GET /api/trips` | browser guest cookie | Reads up to 25 of this browser's saved trips from PostgreSQL. |
| `POST /api/trips` | JSON `plan`, optional edited `itinerary` | Validates dates/preferences, creates a trip, sets a random httpOnly guest cookie, and returns the trip without exposing the cookie identifier. |
| `PATCH /api/trips/{id}` | JSON `itinerary` | Edits an itinerary only when its ID and guest cookie both match. |
| `DELETE /api/trips/{id}` | none | Removes only a trip owned by the guest cookie. |
| `GET /api/nearby?lat=26.92&lng=75.82&kind=hospital` | valid India coordinates and place type | Requests *community-edited*, unverified OpenStreetMap listings. Responds `503` if the volunteer Overpass service fails; the UI offers a clearly labeled external map search. |

A chat request body looks like this:

```json
{
  "messages": [{ "role": "user", "content": "Plan one day in Jaipur" }],
  "destination": "jaipur",
  "language": "en"
}
```

A trip creation request body looks like this:

```json
{
  "plan": {
    "destinations": ["jaipur"],
    "startDate": "2026-11-10",
    "endDate": "2026-11-11",
    "budget": "balanced",
    "accommodation": "flexible",
    "transport": "public",
    "language": "en",
    "interests": ["Architecture"],
    "stepFreeNeeded": false
  }
}
```

Use the `/plan` page for the easiest end-to-end test: choose dates → generate the **illustrative** itinerary → edit a stop → save → reload → open or delete your trip. A guest cookie is **not authentication** and is not cross-device sync. Contacts in the SOS interface are kept in browser storage, not in PostgreSQL. In a real emergency in India, **call 112**; YatraSathi does not send or confirm an emergency dispatch.

## What is real, what is demo, what is not built

- **Working:** destination browsing and filters; cited stories; English/Hindi interface selection; landmark map; browser narration where supported; trip CRUD in PostgreSQL; direct `tel:112` action; consent-based location and prepared share/SMS; live OpenStreetMap lookup *when its public API responds*.
- **Explicit demo:** rule-based Sathi answers without an API key; sample itinerary ordering and budget/mobility prompts. A configured OpenAI key switches chat to a real server-side request, but AI answers must still be checked.
- **Not built:** user accounts, booking and provider verification, live transit, reliable live event feed, verified fare comparisons, background reminders, authorized emergency dispatch, and guaranteed translation or accessibility. Do not demonstrate these as working features.

## If your **other** Windows Vite project gets `EPERM` on `package.json`

The Windows directory in your original description is not mounted inside this sandbox, so these commands are **diagnostics**, not a claim that its permissions were fixed. In the VS Code PowerShell terminal, from that project's own root, run individually:

```powershell
Get-Location
Get-Item .\package.json | Format-List FullName,Attributes,Length,IsReadOnly
icacls .\package.json
icacls .
Get-Content .\package.json
Get-ChildItem . -Name vite.config.*
Get-Process node,npm,Code -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object ProcessName,Id,Path
```

Test whether the **directory** is writable without touching your dependencies:

```powershell
New-Item .\.__write-test -ItemType File -ErrorAction Stop
Remove-Item .\.__write-test
```

If the file alone is read-only, first understand why; `attrib -R .\package.json` removes only that attribute. If the directory test fails, inspect folder ACLs, sync software/OneDrive, antivirus quarantine, and file locks before changing permissions. Do **not** delete `node_modules`, `package-lock.json`, or the project as a first response, and do not assume Administrator mode solves a file lock. Your old React 18 / Vite 5 / `@vitejs/plugin-react` 4 setup should be inspected *in that folder* before proposing version changes; do not add Nodemon for a frontend-only Vite app.

## Learn the current stage

- [Next.js App Router route handlers](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/route-handlers) — how the `.js` backend receives requests.
- [React: State and Effects](https://react.dev/learn) — how the `.jsx` UI works.
- [Drizzle + PostgreSQL](https://orm.drizzle.team/docs/get-started/postgresql-new) — schema, connection, and database queries.

